## 5. Session peer model — singleton persistent peer per MCP session

### [CXC-MCP-SESSION-PEER] MUST treat each MCP session as one singleton persistent peer; MUST support the multi-peer accept-loop model as primary, with a single-process (stdio) session as its degenerate peer-count-1 case

- requirement: (MUST, testable) each MCP session — however its underlying wire is realized
  ([DD-MCP-TRANSPORT-CHOICE]) — MUST be represented by exactly ONE long-lived peer object for the
  lifetime of that session: never re-created per tool call, never pooled. The peer object owns the
  session's tenant binding ([CXC-MCP-TENANT-BOUNDARY]), its derived tool registry
  ([CXC-MCP-ROUTER-MECHANICS]), and any in-flight query correlation state ([CXC-MCP-INVOKE-READ]) for as
  long as the session is open. This is [CXC-WS-SINGLETON-PEER] applied to this connector's own unit of
  connection.
- multi-peer-is-primary: a network-transported MCP surface (SSE, streamable-HTTP) accepts MANY concurrent
  sessions over one listening connector, structurally identical to WS's many-tabs-one-transport shape
  ([CXC-WS-SINGLETON-PEER]'s `claim-many-tabs-is-not-a-new-shape`) — many Intelligences are many
  independent peers, not a new concept.
- stdio-is-degenerate-not-different: a LOCAL stdio-transported MCP session (one server process spawned
  per client, the common desktop-MCP-client shape) is the SAME peer model with peer count fixed at
  exactly one by the transport's own nature — it MUST NOT be implemented as a structurally different peer
  class; the connector's peer-object shape is identical, only the transport's own cardinality differs.
- negative-control: TWO simultaneous MCP sessions (whether two network connections or a network session
  plus a stdio session) MUST produce TWO independent peer objects with independent tenant bindings and
  independent in-flight query state — never one shared mutable peer object silently serving both.
- verify-machine: opening two MCP sessions against a test server (where the transport supports
  concurrency) asserts two distinct peer identities, each with independent tenant binding; closing one
  MUST NOT affect the other's state.
- verify-human: an operator connects two separate MCP clients against a real ComposeServer, binds each to
  a different tenant, and confirms independent tool surfaces and independent in-flight calls.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-recommendation
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MCP]
- see-also: [CXC-WS-SINGLETON-PEER], [CXC-CBK-PERIPHERAL-PLUGIN-MODEL]

