## 6. The plugin tool-declaration contract — the load-bearing deliverable

### [CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION] MUST extend the plugin's typed pydantic manifest with an `mcp_tools` declaration; ONE typed declaration derives the tool's MCP schema too — the author writes none of it by hand

- requirement: (MUST, testable) a plugin declares its MCP surface as a list of tool declarations on the
  SAME typed pydantic model [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT] already uses to derive the plugin's config form and
  announce payload — `mcp_tools: list[McpToolDecl]` (or equivalent), never a second, hand-authored
  registration path. This is [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]'s own rule — "the author never hand-writes the
  form, announce, or registry entry" — extended with a FOURTH derived artifact: the tool's MCP schema.
  Reuse-by-reference, not a fork: [DX-NARROW-WAIST] applied to the manifest itself.
- schema: each `McpToolDecl` MUST carry, at minimum:

| Field | Type | Meaning |
|-------|------|---------|
| `name` | `str` | plugin-LOCAL tool name (unqualified — e.g. `enroll`, not `fingerprint.enroll`) |
| `description` | `str` | human/agent-readable purpose, surfaced verbatim in the MCP tool-list response |
| `input_model` | a pydantic model | the tool's typed arguments; the JSON-schema an MCP client sees is DERIVED from this model, never hand-written separately |
| `direction` | enum: `write` \| `read` \| `read-write` \| `command` | which invocation path this tool uses — [CXC-MCP-INVOKE-WRITE] for `write`, [CXC-MCP-INVOKE-READ] for `read`; `read-write` composes both as an UNCORRELATED publish-then-separate-read (fire-and-forget write, then a separate read); `command` is a MUTATING request whose SINGLE CORRELATED reply carries the authoritative outcome — see `command-direction` below |
| `namespace_mapping` | `{publishes: list[str], subscribes: list[str]}` | the bus namespace(s) a `write` tool call publishes to, and/or the namespace(s) a `read` tool call resolves from — table-driven, per [CXC-MCP-INVOKE-WRITE]/[CXC-MCP-INVOKE-READ]; a `command` tool's SAME `namespace_mapping` shape drives its [SPEC:CXC-BUS-QUERY] round-trip |
| `read_mode` | enum: `retained` \| `query`, required when `direction` includes `read`, and MUST be `query` when `direction` is `command` | `retained` = return the namespace's current value directly (no round-trip, [CXC-MCP-INVOKE-READ]'s direct path); `query` = issue a [SPEC:CXC-BUS-QUERY] round-trip and return its result — the ONLY legal value for `command` (`retained` is invalid for `command` and MUST be rejected at declaration time) |
| `idempotent` | `bool`, default `false` | whether re-invoking with identical args is safe to retry; informational to the client, not enforced by the router |

- command-direction: (MUST, testable) `direction: command` declares a MUTATING request whose SINGLE
  CORRELATED reply carries the authoritative outcome — realized over the [SPEC:CXC-BUS-QUERY] round-trip
  idiom exactly ([CXC-BUS-QUERY-ORDERING]'s subscribe-before-publish rule, [CXC-BUS-QUERY-IDIOM]'s correlate-
  by-`request_id`), so ONE client invocation both MUTATES bus state and RETURNS the authoritative result in
  the same call. This is DISTINCT from `read-write`, which composes an UNCORRELATED publish-then-separate-
  read (fire-and-forget write, then a separate read) and therefore CANNOT carry a single authoritative
  correlated outcome — a `command` tool is never a relabel of convenience for `read-write`, it is a different
  invocation shape. `direction: command` MUST set `read_mode: query`; a declaration pairing `command` with
  `read_mode: retained` (or omitting `read_mode`) MUST be rejected at declaration/load time, never silently
  coerced. G1-CONSISTENT: the connector still only FORWARDS the command request and DECODES the correlated
  reply per [CXC-MCP-INVOKE-READ]'s query path — the MUTATION and the response happen in the plugin's own
  handler, never in this router ([CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-NEVER-TRANSFORMS], [DEC-G1-CONNECTOR-TRANSFORM-BOUNDARY-2026-08-25]).
  A policy/audit/read-only-tenant gate keyed on `direction` MUST classify `command` as a WRITE (`is_write=true`)
  — this is the entire point of the distinction: it closes the hole where a mutating tool mislabeled `read` or
  `read-write` slips past such a gate as non-mutating. Two tools relabel to `command` when their Wave-2b legs
  reconcile: `document_store.store` and `fingerprint_template_store.delete`.
- verify-machine-command: a tool declared `direction: command` produces, for ONE client invocation, exactly
  one subscribe-before-publish/correlate-by-`request_id` round-trip per [CXC-BUS-QUERY-IDIOM] whose single
  reply both confirms the mutation occurred and carries its authoritative result value — never two separate
  calls and never an uncorrelated read; a direction-keyed policy/audit fixture classifying tools by `is_write`
  MUST classify every `command`-declared tool `is_write=true`; a fixture declaring `command` with
  `read_mode: retained` (or no `read_mode`) is rejected at load time, never silently accepted.
- provenance-command-direction: architect-recommendation, implementing [DEC-MCP-COMMAND-DIRECTION-2026-08-25]
  (David authorized the Wave-2b reconcile-and-land including this fix, HANDOVER §1B).

- naming: (MUST, testable) the tool name an MCP client actually sees MUST be
  `{plugin_name}.{tool.name}` — dot-namespaced by the DECLARING PLUGIN's own registered name, mirroring
  [CXC-NSID]'s namespace-identifier discipline one layer up (a semantic string, never a bare unqualified
  tool name that could collide across plugins). Two plugins MAY each declare a tool literally named
  `enroll`; the router's exposed names (`fingerprint.enroll`, `access_card.enroll`) never collide because
  the plugin-name prefix is structural, not a convention a plugin author must remember to apply by hand.
- versioning: (MUST, testable) tool names are UNVERSIONED in v1, exactly as [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR]'s
  capability strings are — a tool rename or a breaking input-schema change is a NEW tool name, and the
  loader's collision/park behaviour (below) is the only guard v1 ships with.
- duplicate-name-parks-loudly: (MUST, testable) if two plugins are ever loaded such that their DERIVED
  exposed names collide (only possible if a plugin's own registered NAME collides — [CXC-CSL-PER-TENANT]'s
  loader already treats that as an error elsewhere), the collision MUST be reported at load/announce time,
  never silently resolved by "first one wins." This mirrors [MODEL-ID]'s "a duplicate id is an error, not
  a merge" discipline extended to this router's own namespace.
- input-schema-is-the-single-derivation-source: (MUST, testable) the JSON-schema an MCP client receives
  for a tool's arguments MUST be generated from `input_model` at announce/list time, never hand-maintained
  separately — the same "form and announce can never drift out of sync with each other" property
  [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT] already states for config, extended to this fourth derived artifact.
- reuse-by-reference: (what a plugin author does NOT write) the MCP JSON-RPC tool-list/tool-call wire
  format itself (owned by the connector, [CXC-MCP-CONNECTOR-INTERFACE]); the publish/query mechanics
  ([CXC-MCP-INVOKE-WRITE]/[CXC-MCP-INVOKE-READ], owned by the router); the announce/re-announce cadence
  ([CXC-CS-DYNAMIC-SURFACE], already built). A plugin author's ENTIRE net-new surface for one tool is:
  one `McpToolDecl` entry naming an `input_model` and a `namespace_mapping`.
- scenario:
  - when: the `fingerprint` plugin declares `McpToolDecl(name="enroll", input_model=EnrollArgs, direction="write", namespace_mapping={"publishes": ["fingerprint/enroll"]})` where `EnrollArgs` is a pydantic model with one field `user_id: int`
  - then: an MCP client sees a tool named `fingerprint.enroll` with a JSON-schema requiring an integer
    `user_id`; invoking it publishes `{"user_id": 5}` to `fingerprint/enroll` on the session's bound
    tenant bus, table-driven from `EnrollArgs`, with no interpretation beyond that mapping
- verify-machine: a plugin declaring one `write` tool and one `read`(`retained`) tool and one
  `read`(`query`) tool, loaded into a test ComposeServer, produces an MCP tool-list carrying exactly those
  three tools under `{plugin}.{name}`, each with a JSON-schema matching its `input_model` field-for-field;
  a duplicate-name fixture produces a loud park/error, never a silent overwrite.
- verify-human: a builder implementing a NEW plugin's MCP surface for the first time (the fan-out this
  contract exists to serve) can do so by writing ONLY the `McpToolDecl` list plus its `input_model`
  classes, confirmed by review against this table with no additional question needing to be asked of the
  architect.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-recommendation
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MCP]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT], [CXC-CS-DYNAMIC-SURFACE], [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR], [DX-NARROW-WAIST], [MODEL-ID], [CXC-MCP-INVOKE-WRITE], [CXC-MCP-INVOKE-READ], [DEC-MCP-COMMAND-DIRECTION-2026-08-25], [DEC-G1-CONNECTOR-TRANSFORM-BOUNDARY-2026-08-25], [CXC-BUS-QUERY-IDIOM]
- naming-for-intelligence: (MUST, david-ruling) a tool's NAME, its parameter names, and its description are named for an INTELLIGENCE consumer: they MUST make the tool's purpose UNAMBIGUOUS to an agent reading the umbrella's tool list, favoring self-describing verbosity over human-API terseness — a longer fully-self-describing name is BETTER, not worse, because the consumer is a Claude instance reading the tool list as its UI, not a human developer with docs and habits. Prefer `enroll_fingerprint_template_for_identity` (+ a description naming what it does and what it publishes) over a bare `enroll`. David 2026-08-25: "all API surfaces need to be named such that their purpose is clear to an Intelligence, even if much longer than a normal human-oriented API surface would be." Full record: [DEC-MCP-SURFACES-NAMED-FOR-INTELLIGENCE-2026-08-25]. The diamond's static leg enforces this on every per-service plugin adaptation.

---

## 7. Tool invocation — encode: a write-tool call is a table-driven publish, never a derive

### [CXC-MCP-INVOKE-WRITE] MUST encode a `write` tool call as a publish onto the tool's declared `namespace_mapping.publishes`, table-driven from `input_model`; MUST NOT derive a value from OTHER bus state

- requirement: (MUST, testable) invoking a `write` tool MUST produce exactly one `BusMessage` publish per
  namespace in `namespace_mapping.publishes`, whose `val` is the tool call's own validated arguments
  (per `input_model`), on the session's currently-bound tenant bus. The router performs NO transformation
  of the argument values beyond what `input_model`'s own pydantic validation/coercion already does
  (type coercion, default-filling) — this is [CXC-MCP-CONNECTOR-NOT-PLUGIN]'s `the-derive-boundary`
  property made concrete: ENCODE is table-driven from the tool's OWN schema, never a lookup or
  computation against ANOTHER namespace's current value.
- boundary: (MUST, testable) if a tool's semantics require combining its own arguments with some OTHER
  namespace's current state before publishing (e.g. "enroll relative to whichever door group is
  currently active"), that combination MUST happen INSIDE the plugin's own handler (subscribing and
  computing on its own bus side) or a `state_manager` peer — never inside this router. A router
  implementation that reads a second namespace to decide what to publish for a `write` tool call is a
  defect against this node, exactly the class [DEC-G1-CONNECTOR-TRANSFORM-BOUNDARY-2026-08-25]'s T1
  temptation ("just parse the frame into meaning right there") names for connectors generally.
- validation-failure: (MUST, testable) arguments that fail `input_model`'s own pydantic validation MUST
  be rejected with a structured MCP tool-call error naming the failing field(s), and MUST NOT be published
  in any partially-coerced form — a validation failure produces zero bus traffic, never a best-effort
  publish.
- verify-machine: invoking a declared `write` tool with valid arguments produces exactly one publish
  matching `input_model`'s validated output on the declared namespace; invoking it with arguments that
  fail validation produces zero bus traffic and a structured error naming the offending field; a
  fixture router that attempts to read a second namespace while encoding a `write` tool fails a
  static/behavioural check asserting single-namespace-touch for that call.
- verify-human: none required beyond the fixture check above — this node's failure surface is
  machine-observable by construction (a `write` tool touching more than its own declared namespace is a
  detectable event, not a judgment call).
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-recommendation
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MCP]
- see-also: [CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION], [DX-CONNECTOR-FORWARDS-NEVER-TRANSFORMS], [DEC-G1-CONNECTOR-TRANSFORM-BOUNDARY-2026-08-25]

### [CXC-MCP-INVOKE-READ] MUST decode a `read` tool call from a retained namespace value directly, OR via the [SPEC:CXC-BUS-QUERY] round-trip when `read_mode: query`

- requirement: (MUST, testable) a `read` tool declared `read_mode: retained` MUST resolve by reading the
  CURRENT value of its declared `namespace_mapping.subscribes` entry/entries directly from the bound
  tenant bus's live state (`bus.namespaces`, the same source [CXC-WS-RETAINED-REPLAY] reads for WS's
  replay-on-subscribe) and returning it as the tool's result — NO publish, no round-trip, no responder
  involved. A namespace with no retained value yet MUST return an explicit "no value yet" result, never a
  fabricated default.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) a `read` tool declared `read_mode: query` MUST invoke [SPEC:CXC-BUS-QUERY]'s
  idiom exactly: subscribe to its own per-session result namespace BEFORE publishing the query
  ([CXC-BUS-QUERY-ORDERING]'s subscribe-before-publish rule), correlate by `request_id`
  ([CXC-BUS-QUERY-IDIOM]), and decode the responder's result `val` VERBATIM into the MCP tool response —
  no re-interpretation of the responder's answer beyond unwrapping the envelope. The MCP session OWNS its
  own timeout ([CXC-BUS-QUERY-ORDERING]'s "timeout/retry is the requester's job"): a query tool call that
  times out MUST surface a structured "no answer" error to the MCP client, never hang the session
  indefinitely.
- boundary: (MUST, testable) decoding MUST NOT summarize, aggregate, or reformat the responder's `val`
  beyond what is needed to fit the MCP tool-result envelope — this is [DEC-G1-CONNECTOR-TRANSFORM-BOUNDARY-2026-08-25]'s
  clarification (2), "decode... recovers the original fact and stops at wire-bytes; interpreting the
  bytes' meaning is a derive," applied to a structured bus answer rather than raw wire bytes.
- verify-machine: a `retained` read tool against a namespace with a known current value returns that
  value byte-identical (modulo envelope unwrapping); against an unset namespace it returns the explicit
  "no value yet" result, never a default. A `query` read tool against a live responder returns the
  responder's `val` verbatim, correlated correctly under >=2 concurrent in-flight calls on the same
  session; against a responder that never answers, the call times out with a named "no answer" error
  rather than hanging.
- verify-human: an operator invokes a `retained` read tool for a namespace they can independently observe
  on the bus (e.g. via a monitor) and confirms the MCP result matches; invokes a `query` read tool against
  a real responder and confirms a timely, correct answer.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-recommendation
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MCP]
- see-also: [CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION], [CXC-BUS-QUERY-IDIOM], [CXC-BUS-QUERY-ORDERING], [CXC-BUS-QUERY-VALIDATION], [CXC-WS-RETAINED-REPLAY], [DEC-G1-CONNECTOR-TRANSFORM-BOUNDARY-2026-08-25]

---

## 8. The thin aggregating router — membrane, not mediator

### [CXC-MCP-ROUTER-MECHANICS] MUST derive its aggregated tool list from the loader's existing derived-announce path; MUST re-derive on plugin re-announce; MUST NEVER cache the tool list as static

- requirement: (MUST, testable) the router's aggregated MCP tool-list MUST be derived from the SAME
  derived-announce payload [CXC-CS-LOADER-PUBLISHES-ANNOUNCE] already publishes to
  `_server/plugins/<name>/announce` on each tenant bus — reading each plugin's `mcp_tools` declaration off
  that existing announce, never standing up a second, parallel announce mechanism just for MCP. This is
  reuse-by-reference ([DX-NARROW-WAIST]) applied to the announce spine, not a new spine.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) on any plugin re-announce (surface change, [CXC-CS-DYNAMIC-SURFACE]), the
  router MUST refresh its aggregated tool list for every currently-BOUND session on that tenant — the
  same on-change discipline [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND] requires generally, applied to this router's own
  cached view. A session's tool-list response MUST NEVER be served from a snapshot taken at bind time and
  never refreshed.
- park-excludes: (MUST, testable) a plugin currently PARKED under [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR]'s unmet-floor
  behaviour MUST NOT contribute any tool to the aggregated list while parked — a parked plugin's tools are
  absent, not present-but-erroring, so an agent's tool-list reflects only what is actually reachable. When
  the plugin unparks (the binder's continuous re-evaluation, [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR]'s
  `dynamic-rebinding`), its tools reappear on the NEXT re-announce-triggered refresh, with no session
  restart required.
- verify-machine: a plugin loaded then re-announced with an ADDED tool causes an already-BOUND session's
  next tool-list call to include the new tool with no reconnect; a plugin PARKED after load contributes
  zero tools to the list; unparking it restores its tools without a session restart.
- verify-human: an operator watches a live MCP client's tool list update after a plugin's config change
  triggers a re-announce, with no client-side reconnect performed.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-recommendation
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MCP]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-LOADER-PUBLISHES-ANNOUNCE], [CXC-CS-DYNAMIC-SURFACE], [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR], [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]

---

## 9. Multi-tenancy — reused, not respecified

### [CXC-MCP-TENANT-BOUNDARY] MUST bind an MCP session to exactly ONE tenant at a time; MUST NEVER let a session's tool calls reach another tenant's plugin instances

- requirement: (MUST, testable) at every instant a session is BOUND, its aggregated tool list and every
  tool invocation resolve against ONLY its currently-selected tenant's plugin instances and bus — this is
  [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY]'s LOCKED-PERMANENTLY "plugins are ALWAYS per-tenant; NO shared instances"
  invariant, applied at this connector's own binding step, exactly as [CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY] applies it
  at the WS connector's and [CXC-CSL-PER-TENANT] applies it at the plugin loader's.
- negative-control: a session BOUND to tenant A invoking a tool name that exists only on tenant B (a
  plugin loaded on B but not A) MUST receive a "tool not found" error — never a cross-tenant dispatch,
  never a silent no-op that could be mistaken for "not implemented yet."
- verify-machine: two tenants each load a plugin registered under the SAME name with different config; a
  session bound to tenant A invoking that plugin's tool touches ONLY A's instance's bus traffic, never
  B's, for the duration it is bound to A.
- verify-human: an operator inspects two tenants' plugin state side by side and confirms an MCP session
  bound to one never surfaces or affects the other's tools or bus traffic.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-recommendation
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MCP]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY], [CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY], [CXC-CSL-PER-TENANT]

---

## 10. The fork-exception — a plugin MAY declare its own MCP endpoint, knowingly

### [CXC-MCP-FORK-EXCEPTION] A plugin whose surface is INHERENTLY separate MAY declare `mcp_endpoint: own`, opting out of the umbrella — a knowing declaration of reduced integration, never the default

- requirement: (MUST, testable) a plugin MAY set `mcp_endpoint: own` on its manifest when its surface is
  genuinely a separate unit — a distinct auth domain, a different tenant boundary than
  [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY] models, or a service that is not itself a ComposeServer plugin at all. Declaring
  `own` MUST cause that plugin's tools to be EXCLUDED from the umbrella's aggregated list — never both
  reachable through the umbrella AND through a separate endpoint, which would let an agent discover the
  same capability two ways with no way to know they are the same capability.
- the-principled-exception: (architect-recommendation, analogous to [DX-REALIZATION-DECLARES-ENVELOPE]'s
  "the realization backend a widget uses IS a declaration of its cross-tier envelope" — the SAME shape of
  rule, not David's word on THIS surface) `mcp_endpoint: own` is a KNOWING declaration that this plugin's
  integration is intentionally reduced, appropriate IFF the plugin is inherently non-aggregable — never a
  convenience escape for a plugin that COULD have joined the umbrella but its author found the umbrella's
  discipline inconvenient. Every umbrella-joinable plugin added shrinks the legitimate use of this
  exception (self-limiting), the same consequence [DX-REALIZATION-DECLARES-ENVELOPE] names for its own
  per-widget escape hatch.
- tell: (MUST NOT, the forbidden case named explicitly) a plugin reaching for `mcp_endpoint: own` because
  its tool-declaration schema ([CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION]) felt awkward to fit, rather than because its
  surface is structurally separate — that is the forbidden fork this exception does not cover.
- default: the ABSENCE of `mcp_endpoint: own` is the default and IS the umbrella path
  ([CXC-MCP-UMBRELLA-RATIFIED]) — a plugin author does nothing extra to join.
- verify-machine: a plugin declaring `mcp_endpoint: own` never appears in the umbrella's aggregated tool
  list under any tenant; a plugin with no such declaration always does (subject to
  [CXC-MCP-ROUTER-MECHANICS]'s park-exclusion).
- verify-human: an architect reviewing a NEW plugin's `mcp_endpoint: own` declaration confirms, by reading
  its manifest, that the plugin is genuinely a separate auth/tenant/service boundary and not merely
  avoiding the umbrella's discipline — a structural/architectural review, not a machine-checkable claim.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-recommendation
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MCP]
- see-also: [CXC-MCP-UMBRELLA-RATIFIED], [DX-REALIZATION-DECLARES-ENVELOPE], [DX-NARROW-WAIST]

---

## 11. The connector interface — what composeio-expert builds

### [CXC-MCP-CONNECTOR-INTERFACE] The concrete module, factory signature, and session-peer object shape

- module: a NEW module, `composeio/transport_mcp.py` — sibling to `transport_ws.py`/`transport_mqtt.py`/
  `transport_can.py`/`transport_uart.py`, registered into `transport_registry.py`'s existing `_FACTORIES`
  exactly as they are (`register("mcp", _mcp)`), imported LAZILY per the registry's own stated discipline
  (a missing optional MCP-server dependency must never break the other registered factories).
- factory-signature: `_mcp(spec: dict, app_id: str) -> MCPConnector`, mirroring `_ws`'s shape
  ([CXC-WS-CONNECTOR-INTERFACE]) — `spec` carries the underlying wire choice and its own config (`stdio`
  needs none beyond process framing; `sse`/`streamable-http` need `host`/`port`/TLS config, per
  [DD-MCP-TRANSPORT-CHOICE]).
- mcp-connector-class: `MCPConnector` — owns the underlying wire's accept surface (however realized) and
  a collection of session-peer objects, one per MCP session ([CXC-MCP-SESSION-PEER]); owns the
  aggregated-tool-list DERIVATION from each tenant's plugin loader announce
  ([CXC-MCP-ROUTER-MECHANICS]). Its public surface: `sessions` (enumerating live peers, for status/debug
  surfaces, mirroring [CXC-CSL-STATUS-SURFACE]'s pattern), and a `tool_registry(tenant_id)` accessor a
  session peer's tool-list call resolves against.
- mcp-peer-class: `MCPPeer` (or equivalent) — one instance per session, owning: `state`
  (`PRE-BINDING` | `BOUND`, [CXC-MCP-ENDPOINT-TOPOLOGY]); `bound_tenant: str | None`; in-flight
  `request_id -> pending call` correlation state for `read`(`query`) tools
  ([CXC-MCP-INVOKE-READ]/[CXC-BUS-QUERY-IDIOM]). It exposes `select_tenant(tenant_id)`,
  `list_tools()`, `invoke(tool_qualified_name, args)`, and `close()`.
- reuse-by-reference: (what this module does NOT reimplement) [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]'s pydantic
  form/announce/registry derivation, extended in-place for `mcp_tools` rather than forked
  ([CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION]); [SPEC:CXC-BUS-QUERY]'s request/response idiom, verbatim
  (`CXC-BUS-QUERY-IDIOM`/`-ORDERING`/`-VALIDATION`); `bus.namespaces`/`Namespace.value` for
  `retained`-mode reads, the same accessor [CXC-WS-RETAINED-REPLAY] uses; per-tenant bus resolution
  (`ComposeServer`'s existing multi-tenant orchestration, [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY]). This module's entire
  net-new surface is: the session accept/spawn path for whichever wire [DD-MCP-TRANSPORT-CHOICE] settles,
  per-session peer state and tenant-selection transitions, the encode/decode dispatch of
  [CXC-MCP-INVOKE-WRITE]/[CXC-MCP-INVOKE-READ], and the tool-list aggregation of
  [CXC-MCP-ROUTER-MECHANICS].
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-recommendation
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MCP]
- see-also: [CXC-WS-CONNECTOR-INTERFACE], [CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION], [CXC-BUS-QUERY-IDIOM], [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY]

---

## 12. Explicit out-of-scope — cited, not specified

### [CXC-MCP-OUT-OF-SCOPE] The units this spec deliberately does not design

- the-underlying-wire: the concrete stdio/SSE/streamable-HTTP transport realization underneath the MCP
  protocol layer is [DD-MCP-TRANSPORT-CHOICE], OPEN — this spec specifies only that it is swappable
  connector config, per [plt.cio:SPEC:DP-CIO-6].
- the-mcp-action-widget-binding: [SPEC:CIO-UX-MCPACTION] (the ComposeUX `mcp_action` binding type) is a
  STRUCTURALLY DIFFERENT concern — a ComposeUX WIDGET invoking SOME MCP server's tool and rendering its
  result. This spec is the PRODUCER side (ComposeServer exposing plugin tools as an MCP server); that spec
  is a potential CONSUMER of some MCP server's tools, this umbrella included, but the two are not the same
  surface and this spec does not respecify or extend it. Cited only so the two `MCP`-named surfaces are
  never conflated.
- the-per-service-adaptations: the concrete `McpToolDecl` lists each future plugin (fingerprint, time,
  access control, etc.) will declare are the fan-out this spec exists to make POSSIBLE, not to enumerate —
  each is its own dispatch against [CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION].
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-recommendation, restating the dispatch's explicit scope split as a queryable node
  rather than leaving it only in frontmatter prose.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MCP]
- see-also: [SPEC:CIO-UX-MCPACTION], [DD-MCP-TRANSPORT-CHOICE], [DD-MCP-AUTH-GATE]

---

## 13. Error and boundary matrix

### [CXC-MCP-BOUNDARIES] Every condition a builder would otherwise have to invent an answer for

| # | Condition | Required behaviour |
|---|-----------|--------------------|
| 1 | Unknown `type:` (not `mcp`) reaches this connector's registration path | N/A to this connector — `transport_registry.build()` already raises `UnknownTransportError` naming `known_types()` ([CXC-MCP-CONNECTOR-NOT-PLUGIN]) |
| 2 | A tool call arrives while the session is `PRE-BINDING` (no tenant selected) | rejected with a named error requiring tenant selection first; never dispatched against a default tenant ([CXC-MCP-ENDPOINT-TOPOLOGY]) |
| 3 | A tool-qualified name (`plugin.tool`) does not exist on the bound tenant | "tool not found" error; never a cross-tenant lookup even if the name exists elsewhere ([CXC-MCP-TENANT-BOUNDARY]) |
| 4 | Arguments fail `input_model` validation | structured error naming the failing field(s); zero bus traffic produced ([CXC-MCP-INVOKE-WRITE]) |
| 5 | A `write` tool's declared plugin is currently PARKED (unmet `requires:` floor) | the tool is already absent from the tool list ([CXC-MCP-ROUTER-MECHANICS]); an invocation of a stale cached name from before the park returns a "tool not found" error, never a silent no-op |
| 6 | A `query`-mode read tool's responder never answers | the call times out with a named "no answer" error, on the SESSION's own timeout — the router never hangs indefinitely ([CXC-MCP-INVOKE-READ], reusing [CXC-BUS-QUERY-ORDERING]'s requester-owns-timeout rule) |
| 7 | A `retained`-mode read tool's namespace has never been published | explicit "no value yet" result, never a fabricated default ([CXC-MCP-INVOKE-READ]) |
| 8 | Two plugins' derived exposed tool names collide | loud load/announce-time error, never silent first-wins ([CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION]) |
| 9 | A plugin declares `mcp_endpoint: own` | excluded entirely from the umbrella's aggregated list on every tenant ([CXC-MCP-FORK-EXCEPTION]) |
| 10 | A plugin re-announces mid-session with an added/removed tool | already-BOUND sessions' NEXT tool-list call reflects the change; no reconnect required ([CXC-MCP-ROUTER-MECHANICS]) |
| 11 | An untrusted network can reach the MCP listen surface (network-transported mode, [DD-MCP-TRANSPORT-CHOICE]: DECIDED streamable-HTTP for v1) | MUST bind the trusted LAN only — never `0.0.0.0`/`::`/empty/world-facing. This is OUTSIDE this spec's v1 access-control surface entirely — [DD-MCP-AUTH-GATE]'s invalidating_assumption fires the moment the surface is network-reachable, and with no auth in v1 the LAN-only bind is the ONLY thing standing between the listen surface and an untrusted network. Enforced in code by `UnsafeBindError`, refusing any of `""`/`"0.0.0.0"`/`"::"`/`"*"` at bind time, never a silent fallback to a world-facing default. The deferred auth gate must still land before any exposure beyond the trusted LAN ships. Per [DEC-MCP-DD-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]. |
| 12 | Two concurrent `query`-mode read calls are in flight on the SAME session | correlated independently by `request_id` per [CXC-BUS-QUERY-IDIOM]; neither call's result is ever delivered to the other's caller |

- lan-bind-is-a-must-not-a-should: (added 2026-08-25, [DEC-MCP-DD-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]) row 11 is stated as
  a MUST, not a SHOULD or an aspiration — the streamable-HTTP wire decision at [DD-MCP-TRANSPORT-CHOICE]
  made this guard load-bearing rather than a hypothetical future concern, because v1 ships with NO auth
  ([DD-MCP-AUTH-GATE]). This mirrors the current broker/WS flat-subnet posture
  ([DEC-(a private host name, not published)-STAYS-ON-LOCAL-SUBNET]).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MCP]

---

## 14. Acceptance — machine signal + glass gate

### [CXC-MCP-ACCEPTANCE] The umbrella's two-tier verdict — a machine signal AND, for every failure class human eyes can catch that a narrow test cannot, an explicit human/glass gate

- definition: per [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE] and the WS-spec precedent ([CXC-WS-ACCEPTANCE]),
  machine-green alone never accepts this connector — a cross-tenant tool leak or a tool that silently
  derives instead of encoding are exactly the failure classes a narrow unit test can pass while the real
  agent experience is broken. [CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS] applies throughout this table.

| ID | Requirement | verify-machine | verify-human (glass) |
|----|-------------|-----------------|------------------------|
| MCP-V1 | Registered as `type: mcp` connector, never a plugin ([CXC-MCP-CONNECTOR-NOT-PLUGIN]) | `known_types()` includes `mcp`; `build({"type":"mcp",...})` returns a connector, never silently `None` on a valid spec | none required |
| MCP-V2 | ONE umbrella aggregates every loaded plugin's tools ([CXC-MCP-UMBRELLA-RATIFIED]) | >=2 plugins' tools all reachable over ONE session, no second connection needed | none required |
| MCP-V3 | Tenant-scoped per session, PRE-BINDING until selection ([CXC-MCP-ENDPOINT-TOPOLOGY]) | no tenant-scoped tool reachable pre-selection; tools resolve to the selected tenant only | operator confirms no tool from an unselected tenant is ever offered or invokable |
| MCP-V4 | One tenant at a time, no cross-tenant tool dispatch ([CXC-MCP-TENANT-BOUNDARY]) | same-named tool on two tenants never cross-dispatches for a bound session | operator inspects two tenants side by side and confirms no leak |
| MCP-V5 | Tool schema is DERIVED from `input_model`, never hand-duplicated ([CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION]) | tool-list JSON-schema matches `input_model` field-for-field for a fixture plugin | a builder implementing a new plugin's tools needs no question answered outside this table |
| MCP-V6 | `write` tool call is table-driven encode, never a derive from other state ([CXC-MCP-INVOKE-WRITE]) | fixture asserting single-namespace-touch per `write` call; validation failure produces zero bus traffic | none required — machine-observable by construction |
| MCP-V7 | `read` tool call resolves retained-direct or via [SPEC:CXC-BUS-QUERY], decoded verbatim ([CXC-MCP-INVOKE-READ]) | retained read matches known current value byte-identical; query read round-trips correctly under >=2 concurrent in-flight calls; unanswered query times out named, never hangs | operator confirms a query-mode tool against real infrastructure returns a timely, correct answer |
| MCP-V8 | Router refreshes on re-announce, never serves a static snapshot ([CXC-MCP-ROUTER-MECHANICS]) | added/removed tool reflected on next tool-list call with no reconnect; parked plugin contributes zero tools | operator watches a live client's tool list update after a config-driven re-announce |
| MCP-V9 | Fork-exception excludes an `own`-declared plugin from the umbrella entirely ([CXC-MCP-FORK-EXCEPTION]) | such a plugin never appears in the aggregated list on any tenant | architect review confirms the declaration is a genuine structural boundary, not convenience |
| MCP-V10 | v1 ships with no auth, position reserved not foreclosed ([DD-MCP-AUTH-GATE]); MUST bind the trusted LAN only, never `0.0.0.0`/world-facing, until the deferred gate lands ([CXC-MCP-BOUNDARIES] row 11, [DEC-MCP-DD-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]) | no login/account code path exists; the tenant-selection seam is the one a gate would attach to; a bind attempt with host in `{"", "0.0.0.0", "::", "*"}` raises `UnsafeBindError` and never silently starts listening | operator confirms the deployed MCP listen surface is bound to a specific trusted-LAN interface (COMPOSEIO_MQTT_BROKER's (a private address, not published) subnet), never `0.0.0.0` or any world-reachable address |
| MCP-V11 | Placement recorded, not silent ([CXC-MCP-PLACEMENT]) | the spec carries the `[PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED]` node + the filename-check property | n/a — structural |

- requirement: MCP-V4 (the cross-tenant boundary) is the gate for [CXC-MCP-TENANT-BOUNDARY] — a bound
  session reaching another tenant's plugin instance AT ANY INSTANT is UNSAFE and MUST NOT ship, because
  [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY] is LOCKED PERMANENTLY, mirroring [CXC-WS-ACCEPTANCE]'s WS-V4 gate discipline
  exactly.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MCP]
- see-also: [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE], [CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS], [CXC-WS-ACCEPTANCE]

---

## 15. Evidence — pre-integration questions answered, post-integration slots EMPTY until a real build

### [CXC-MCP-EVIDENCE] Pre-integration questions (answered by this design pass) and post-integration evidence (EMPTY — never run)

- pre-integration questions answered by this spec: (1) plugin-vs-connector -> connector, new
  `transport_registry.py` factory, [CXC-MCP-CONNECTOR-NOT-PLUGIN]; (2) umbrella-vs-per-plugin -> ONE
  aggregating umbrella, ratified by David's concurrence, [CXC-MCP-UMBRELLA-RATIFIED]; (3) endpoint
  topology -> one per ComposeServer, tenant-scoped per session (architect's lean, alternative recorded),
  [CXC-MCP-ENDPOINT-TOPOLOGY]; (4) session peer model -> singleton persistent peer, multi-peer primary,
  stdio degenerate, [CXC-MCP-SESSION-PEER]; (5) tool declaration shape -> a fourth derived artifact on
  the existing pydantic plugin manifest, [CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION]; (6) write-tool mechanics -> table-driven
  publish, never derive, [CXC-MCP-INVOKE-WRITE]; (7) read-tool mechanics -> direct retained read OR
  [SPEC:CXC-BUS-QUERY] round-trip, decoded verbatim, [CXC-MCP-INVOKE-READ]; (8) router mechanics -> reuse
  the loader's existing derived-announce, never a second spine, [CXC-MCP-ROUTER-MECHANICS]; (9) tenant
  boundary -> reused verbatim from [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY]/[CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY], [CXC-MCP-TENANT-BOUNDARY];
  (10) fork-exception -> `mcp_endpoint: own`, knowing and exclusive, [CXC-MCP-FORK-EXCEPTION].
- pre-integration questions LEFT open, flagged for David/architect ratify before or during build (not
  silently resolved): the endpoint-topology alternative ([DD-MCP-ENDPOINT-PER-TENANT]); the underlying
  wire transport ([DD-MCP-TRANSPORT-CHOICE]); the auth gate's own concrete mechanism
  ([DD-MCP-AUTH-GATE], deferred by design).
- evidence-connector-module: EMPTY — `composeio/transport_mcp.py` does not exist on `origin/main` as of
  this spec's authoring (2026-08-25); this is a design-review spec, not a report of landed code.
- evidence-tool-declaration-real-plugin: EMPTY — no real plugin has declared `mcp_tools` against
  [CXC-MCP-TOOL-DECLARATION]; the fingerprint/enroll example in that node's `scenario` property is
  illustrative, not evidenced.
- evidence-multi-tenant-real-infra: EMPTY — no two-tenant deployment has been observed to confirm
  MCP-V4's no-cross-tenant-dispatch human gate.
- evidence-query-round-trip-real-load: EMPTY — no real MCP client has exercised a `query`-mode read tool
  against a live [SPEC:CXC-BUS-QUERY] responder over real infrastructure.
- evidence-router-refresh-real-reload: EMPTY — no real plugin re-announce has been observed to refresh a
  live MCP client's tool list without a reconnect.
- why-empty-matters: per [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE], "never run at all" must be a QUERYABLE PROPERTY,
  not something a reader has to already know — every slot above is `EMPTY` explicitly rather than simply
  absent, so a future query against this node can distinguish "not yet built" from "nobody thought to
  record it."
- status: draft
- status-detail: draft — every evidence slot EMPTY by construction; this spec has not been implemented
- provenance: architect-recommendation
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MCP]
- see-also: [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE], [CXC-MCP-ACCEPTANCE]

