## 16. Open forks — flagged for the architect's / David's ratify

### [DD-MCP-ENDPOINT-PER-TENANT] DECIDED — rejected for v1; umbrella-per-server chosen

- question: [CXC-MCP-ENDPOINT-TOPOLOGY] leans toward ONE MCP connector per ComposeServer with
  post-connect tenant selection. The defensible alternative: one MCP endpoint PER TENANT (a distinct
  listen surface, or a distinct stdio spawn target, per tenant), skipping the selection step entirely
  because the endpoint itself IS the tenant boundary.
- why-left-open: this was the "genuinely defensible alternative" the dispatch explicitly asked to be
  surfaced rather than silently decided. Both shapes satisfy [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY]'s LOCKED boundary;
  they differ in WHERE the tenant boundary is enforced (a connection-time selection step vs. the
  endpoint's own identity) and in operational shape (one thing to stand up and monitor vs. N). Now
  resolved — see `decision` below.
- candidates: (a) umbrella-per-ComposeServer, tenant selected post-connect (CHOSEN for v1) — fewer
  moving parts to deploy/monitor, matches [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]'s already-settled shape, but a session must
  complete a selection step before doing anything tenant-scoped; (b) endpoint-per-tenant (REJECTED for
  v1) — an MCP client configured for tenant A can never accidentally reach for tenant B (the boundary is
  structural, not a runtime state to get wrong), but N tenants means N listen surfaces/spawn targets to
  provision, and a cross-tenant operator view (already kernel-owned per [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY]) would need
  its own aggregation across those N endpoints rather than one session's tenant-switch.
- decision: DECIDED — rejected for v1; umbrella-per-server chosen (max reuse of WS lifecycle + loader
  announce spine). RE-LITIGABLE per this node's own invalidating_assumption if real multi-tenant use shows
  agents hold one long-lived binding. Per [DEC-MCP-DD-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]: candidate (a) wins on maximal
  reuse — it reuses [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]'s already-settled binding lifecycle, the loader's derived-announce
  spine ([CXC-CS-LOADER-PUBLISHES-ANNOUNCE]), and one kernel-level socket
  ([CXC-MCP-CONNECTOR-NOT-PLUGIN] socket-scope), rather than forking a divergent per-tenant model that
  would also need its own cross-tenant aggregation; cross-tenant isolation is enforced structurally by
  [CXC-MCP-TENANT-BOUNDARY] + the MCP-V4 acceptance gate; and it degenerates cleanly to the single-tenant
  dev bus deployed first.
- rationale: see `why-left-open` and `candidates` above.
- alternatives: see `candidates` above.
- invalidating_assumption: once real multi-tenant MCP deployments exist and it becomes clear whether
  agents typically hold ONE long-lived tenant binding (favoring (b)) or SWITCH tenants within a session
  (favoring (a), matching how an operator console switches WS focal buses), this fork should resolve from
  observed use rather than from the abstract trade-off alone. This is the RE-LITIGATION TRIGGER for the
  DECIDED choice above, not merely historical color.
- status: decided
- status-detail: decided — rejected for v1 per [DEC-MCP-DD-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]; re-litigable per
  `invalidating_assumption` above, not indefinitely closed.
- provenance: architect-proposed — the fork was named explicitly per the dispatch's own instruction to
  surface a defensible alternative rather than silently choose; the candidate trade-off analysis was this
  spec-writer's own addition. Resolved as ARCHITECT-RECOMMENDATION per [DEC-MCP-DD-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]
  (David delegated the mechanism ratify).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MCP]
- see-also: [CXC-MCP-ENDPOINT-TOPOLOGY], [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY], [DEC-MCP-DD-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]

### [DD-MCP-TRANSPORT-CHOICE] DECIDED — streamable-HTTP for v1

- question: [CXC-MCP-CONNECTOR-NOT-PLUGIN] states the wire underneath MCP's JSON-RPC is swappable
  connector config — it does not pick one. Common MCP wire realizations: stdio (one process per client,
  local-only), SSE, or streamable-HTTP (both network-capable, multi-session).
- why-left-open: `MCPPeer`'s public surface ([CXC-MCP-CONNECTOR-INTERFACE]) does not require the wire to
  be decided to be implementable and testable — the peer model, tool dispatch, and tenant boundary are
  all wire-agnostic by construction ([CXC-MCP-SESSION-PEER]'s stdio-is-degenerate-not-different property
  exists precisely so this fork did not block the rest of the spec). Now resolved — see `decision` below.
- candidates: (a) stdio only — simplest, matches the most common desktop-MCP-client integration today,
  but local-only (no remote agent, no multi-session accept loop, [CXC-MCP-SESSION-PEER]'s multi-peer case
  never exercised); (b) streamable-HTTP (CHOSEN for v1) — network-capable, multi-session natively, closest
  in shape to [SPEC:CXC-WS]'s own accept-loop model, reuses the most WS-connector mechanics; (c) both,
  chosen per deployment via `spec` config — most flexible, most surface to build and test; not needed for
  v1 since the wire stays swappable connector config.
- decision: DECIDED — streamable-HTTP for v1 (networked hosted service serving remote Intelligences);
  stdio is the degenerate-supported case; wire stays swappable per [plt.cio:SPEC:DP-CIO-6]. Per
  [DEC-MCP-DD-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]: ComposeServer is a networked, long-running hosted service serving
  REMOTE/hosted Intelligences (this deployment's own hosted-child pattern), not local desktop MCP clients;
  streamable-HTTP is network-capable, multi-session-native, and closest in shape to [SPEC:CXC-WS]'s own
  accept-loop, reusing the most WS-connector mechanics. stdio remains the degenerate-supported local case
  ([CXC-MCP-SESSION-PEER]'s `stdio-is-degenerate-not-different`) but is NOT the v1 build target.
- rationale: see `why-left-open` above.
- alternatives: see `candidates` above.
- invalidating_assumption: once it is clear whether the primary MCP clients this surface serves are
  local desktop agents (favoring stdio) or remote/hosted Intelligences (favoring streamable-HTTP, and
  matching this deployment's own hosted-child pattern, [66-hosted-child-quiescence.lts]), the choice
  should follow that usage — this is why streamable-HTTP was chosen for v1, not decided in the abstract.
- status: decided
- status-detail: decided — streamable-HTTP for v1 per [DEC-MCP-DD-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]; also the guard
  that makes the LAN-only-bind requirement load-bearing — see [DD-MCP-AUTH-GATE] and
  [CXC-MCP-BOUNDARIES] row 11.
- provenance: architect-proposed — the fork was named explicitly because this spec's own frontmatter
  scope commits only to "transport is an implementation detail," not to a specific wire. Resolved as
  ARCHITECT-RECOMMENDATION per [DEC-MCP-DD-RESOLVED-2026-08-25] (David delegated the mechanism ratify).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MCP]
- see-also: [CXC-MCP-CONNECTOR-INTERFACE], [CXC-MCP-SESSION-PEER], [DD-MCP-AUTH-GATE], [DEC-MCP-DD-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]

### [DD-MCP-AUTH-GATE] DEFERRED — auth is OUT of v1; the position is reserved, not designed

- question: what identity/authorization mechanism gates an MCP session before it can select a tenant or
  invoke a tool.
- why-left-open: mirrors [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE] exactly — v1 ships with no auth, and the seam a future gate
  would attach to (the tenant-selection step, [CXC-MCP-ENDPOINT-TOPOLOGY]) is named so a later gate does
  not have to be retrofitted into a structure that never anticipated it.
- candidates: not enumerated here — belongs to the auth gate's own future design pass, exactly as
  [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE] leaves its own candidates unenumerated.
- decision: DEFERRED — out of v1 by design, not an open lean awaiting a choice.
- rationale: see `why-left-open` above.
- alternatives: not enumerated here.
- invalidating_assumption: the moment this surface is reachable over an untrusted network (network-
  transported mode under [DD-MCP-TRANSPORT-CHOICE]), the deferred gate must be resolved before that
  exposure ships — see [CXC-MCP-BOUNDARIES] row 11.
- streamable-http-makes-the-guard-load-bearing: [DD-MCP-TRANSPORT-CHOICE] is now DECIDED — v1 ships
  streamable-HTTP, a network-transported wire, not stdio. Per [DEC-MCP-DD-RESOLVED-2026-08-25], that
  choice makes this node's `invalidating_assumption` immediately live rather than a hypothetical future
  case: with no auth in v1, the ONLY thing standing between this surface and an untrusted network is the
  LAN-only-bind guard now named explicitly at [CXC-MCP-BOUNDARIES] row 11 and MCP-V10
  ([CXC-MCP-ACCEPTANCE]) — the MCP listen surface MUST bind the trusted LAN only (never `0.0.0.0`/
  world-facing), enforced in code by `UnsafeBindError`, until this deferred gate lands. This mirrors the
  current broker/WS flat-subnet posture ([DEC-(a private host name, not published)-STAYS-ON-LOCAL-SUBNET]).
- status: deferred
- status-detail: settled-deferred, not an open fork awaiting a lean — matches [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE]'s own
  status-detail exactly; CONFIRMED deferred for v1 by [DEC-MCP-DD-RESOLVED-2026-08-25], WITH the
  LAN-only-bind guard above now load-bearing rather than aspirational.
- provenance: architect-recommendation — no equivalent David ruling exists for THIS surface specifically;
  the deferral posture is carried over from [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE]'s settled shape by analogy, flagged as such
  rather than presented as a fresh David ruling on MCP. Confirmed deferred (with the LAN-bind guard) per
  [DEC-MCP-DD-RESOLVED-2026-08-25].
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MCP]
- see-also: [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE], [CXC-MCP-ENDPOINT-TOPOLOGY], [CXC-MCP-BOUNDARIES], [DD-MCP-TRANSPORT-CHOICE], [DEC-MCP-DD-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]

